How percentages are being measured
- Core PCI MSI/MSI-X infrastructure:
  - capability discovery
  - vector allocation and release
  - MSI programming
  - MSI-X table programming
  - fallback MSI-X -> MSI -> INTx
- Driver adoption:
  - production drivers requesting vectors via pci_irq_alloc_vectors
  - mode-aware irq_register and teardown
- Interrupt-path integration quality:
  - no INTx assumptions in common helpers
  - consistent mode-aware behavior in probe/error paths
- Observability/debug surface:
  - ability to see per-device irq mode and vectors in debug/proc output
- Validation coverage:
  - runtime sanity checks and repeated attach/failure path confidence

What is already in place
- MSI and MSI-X capability parsing and programming are implemented in kernel/driver/pci.c.
- pci_irq_alloc_vectors, pci_irq_free_vectors, pci_irq_vector, pci_irq_mode are exported in include/pci.h.
- Trap/IRQ dispatch path can handle dynamically registered vectors across full IRQ range.

Current blockers (code-verified)
- QEMU default machine model still exposes INTx behavior in guest-visible runtime (`/proc/pci` shows `I:1`), so MSI/MSI-X runtime evidence remains limited in this environment.
- NVMe intentionally disables MSI/MSI-X and operates polled-only.

